<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Qins</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/206-bce">206</a>–<a href="/years/205-bce">205 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qins</a> were the kingdoms of Yong, Sai and Di created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> in 206 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a>, the old heartland of Qin, for the surrendered Qin generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-xin">Sima Xin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-yi">Dong Yi</a>. Intended to block <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a>, they were conquered by him within months, opening the Chu–Han contention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-qins.jpg" alt="Three Qins" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nederlandse Leeuw · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eighteen_Kingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
